{{if {{.active_affinity == anabasis_in}} }}{{setvar::active_affinity_flavor::内登 (uchinobori) / 向内法 (xiàngnèi fǎ) (anabasis_in)
 etymology: the inward axis; cf. Gk. eisō (within) + Jp. nobori 登り (climb)
 use: halve the focal distance with each sentence; do not skip a level; the final clause must sit smaller than the body, smaller than the hand, smaller than the cuticle
 looks like: The harbor. The dockside. His hands on the rope. The cuticle bitten too deep to be from today.
 failure: the inward walk that does not end inside the body collapses into cinematic glide; if the last sentence is still room-sized, the descent was never made
 operator feel: the room halving itself around you sentence by sentence}}{{setvar::active_affinity_response::I will work in 内登 (uchinobori).}}{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_affinity == anabasis_out}} }}{{setvar::active_affinity_flavor::外登 (sotonobori) / 向外法 (xiàngwài fǎ) (anabasis_out)
 etymology: the outward axis; cf. Gk. exō (outside) + Jp. sotonobori 外登り (climb-out)
 use: begin at the smallest possible scale, breath on skin or thumbnail crack, then double the frame with each sentence; the character must shrink into context and that shrinking must carry weight
 looks like: The crack in her thumbnail. The hand. The woman at the bus stop. The avenue behind her. The city under low cloud. A coast no one had named yet from this distance.
 failure: outward steps that do not earn the smallness deliver a postcard; the final wide frame must answer the opening detail, not abandon it
 operator feel: the body becoming a speck while the page keeps watching}}{{setvar::active_affinity_response::I will work in 外登 (sotonobori).}}{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_affinity == anabasis_kireji}} }}{{setvar::active_affinity_flavor::切れ登 (kire-nobori) / 斷登法 (duàndēng fǎ) (anabasis_kireji)
 etymology: Jp. kireji 切れ字 (cutting-word) + nobori 登り (climb); the cut that severs the ascent
 use: telescope inward across the paragraph, getting closer and closer, then deliver ONE hard sentence at the widest possible frame; the cut must occur on a sentence break, not within a sentence
 looks like: The kitchen. The table. His knuckles on the wood. The hair on the back of one finger. The whole valley lay under a sky that did not know him.
 failure: softening the cut with a transitional clause kills the whiplash; if the wide frame is foreshadowed, the kireji has been smoothed into a pan
 operator feel: inches from a face, and then the valley}}{{setvar::active_affinity_response::I will work in 切れ登 (kire-nobori).}}{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_affinity == dhvani_drift_free}} }}{{setvar::active_affinity_flavor::自由染 (jiyū-sen) / 自由間接 (zìyóu jiānjiē) (dhvani_drift_free)
 etymology: Jp. jiyū 自由 (free) + sen 染 (dye); free indirect discourse re-read through the staining metaphor
 use: begin a sentence in objective narration and end it inside a character's head; no italics, no 'they thought,' no quotation; the vocabulary and rhythm of the second half must belong to the character, not the narrator
 looks like: She crossed the lobby at a normal pace. The carpet was the carpet of a hotel that wanted to be older than it was, and who did they think they were fooling, the brass was plastic.
 failure: if the seam is visible, the technique has not fired; a tag, a pivot word, or a tonal stutter at the join announces the move and ruins it
 operator feel: you were watching her and now you are her and you cannot point to the door you walked through}}{{setvar::active_affinity_response::I will work in 自由染 (jiyū-sen).}}{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_affinity == dhvani_drift_lex}} }}{{setvar::active_affinity_flavor::語染 (gosen) / 詞染法 (císǎn fǎ) (dhvani_drift_lex)
 etymology: Jp. go 語 / Ch. cí 詞 (word, lexicon) + 染 (dye); contamination by vocabulary
 use: describe a person or object with word choices that belong to the POV character's bias, not to a neutral narrator; hostility lingers in adjective choice, attraction lingers in the details selected; never announce the bias
 looks like: He arrived with the same expensive coat, draped, as always, just so, and explained, again, with the small smile of a man explaining, why the meeting could not be moved.
 failure: an overt evaluative word ('smug,' 'awful') burns the contamination back to plain editorial; the bias must live in selection and modifier, not in verdict
 operator feel: the narration has an opinion and you absorbed it before you noticed}}{{setvar::active_affinity_response::I will work in 語染 (gosen).}}{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_affinity == dhvani_drift_dual}} }}{{setvar::active_affinity_flavor::双染 (sōsen) / 雙染法 (shuāngrǎn fǎ) (dhvani_drift_dual)
 etymology: Jp./Ch. 双 / 雙 (sō, shuāng, 'paired, twin') + 染 (dye); the two-perspective bleed
 use: compose a paragraph that begins inside one character's interiority and ends inside another's; the seam between them must be invisible; biases of both must be detectable in the same sentences, and the operator must untangle whose thought belongs to whom
 looks like: The kitchen was too warm, the way kitchens get when someone has been waiting in them too long, and from the doorway she looked like a stranger pretending to be at ease, like a woman who had walked too far to turn around now.
 failure: alternating sentences between heads is not dual dyeing; that is just a switching POV; the bleed must occur inside a sentence or across an invisible join, with both consciousnesses tinting the same writing
 operator feel: two minds in one paragraph and the air between them}}{{setvar::active_affinity_response::I will work in 双染 (sōsen).}}{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_affinity == fu_catalog_owned}} }}{{setvar::active_affinity_flavor::所持賦 (shoji-fu) / 持物賦 (chíwù fù) (fu_catalog_owned)
 etymology: Jp. shoji 所持 / Ch. chíwù 持物 (held things, possessions) + fu 賦
 use: characterize a person this cycle by what is on their desk, in their bag, in their pockets; describe quality, wear, and arrangement of the objects, not the person; the operator infers the owner from the manifest
 looks like: Three pens, all uncapped. A receipt folded twice. The phone face-down. A pair of reading glasses with one arm taped at the hinge. A single almond in the bottom of the bag, salted, old.
 failure: naming objects and then explaining what they mean about the owner collapses the figure; the gloss must be withheld, or the inventory has done none of the work
 operator feel: knowing a stranger from their drawer}}{{setvar::active_affinity_response::I will work in 所持賦 (shoji-fu).}}{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_affinity == fu_catalog_room}} }}{{setvar::active_affinity_flavor::室賦 (shitsu-fu) / 室賦法 (shìfù fǎ) (fu_catalog_room)
 etymology: Jp. shitsu 室 / Ch. shì 室 (room) + fu 賦
 use: let a room speak for its occupant; catalog what is worn, what is new, what has been repaired, what has been given up on; the contradictions between categories carry the characterization
 looks like: The sofa had been reupholstered once, badly. A new kettle sat beside a stove with two working burners out of four. A stack of unopened mail. A cactus, watered.
 failure: a tidy room described tidily collapses into set dressing; the inventory must carry contradiction (new beside broken, kept beside abandoned) or it does not characterize
 operator feel: the person is in the next room and you already know what they will be like}}{{setvar::active_affinity_response::I will work in 室賦 (shitsu-fu).}}{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_affinity == fu_catalog_shared}} }}{{setvar::active_affinity_flavor::共賦 (kyō-fu) / 共物賦 (gòngwù fù) (fu_catalog_shared)
 etymology: Jp. kyō 共 / Ch. gòng 共 (shared, common) + fu 賦
 use: show a relationship through the objects two people share, half-share, or have stopped sharing; the second toothbrush, the cleared drawer, the unmoved mug; the inventory is the relationship's current state
 looks like: Two toothbrushes in the cup. One drawer empty since April. The mug with the chipped handle still on the left side of the sink, where she used to set it down.
 failure: narrating the relationship's history around the object explains what the object was supposed to prove; the catalog must stand alone, untranslated
 operator feel: the relationship is a shape made of things, and you can see the shape}}{{setvar::active_affinity_response::I will work in 共賦 (kyō-fu).}}{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_affinity == antiphonal_clipped}} }}{{setvar::active_affinity_flavor::裏切音 (uragiri-on) / 短裏潮 (duǎnlǐ cháo) (antiphonal_clipped)
 etymology: Jp. uragiri 裏切り (betrayal, lit. 'cut from behind') + on 音 (sound); the clipped under-tide
 use: write a scene whose surface content is calm or pleasant using short, mechanical, clipped sentences; the rhythm must feel wrong; the content says 'fine,' the rhythm says 'no,' and neither register is allowed to acknowledge the other
 looks like: The kitchen was warm. She had made tea. He sat. He drank the tea. He thanked her. The clock did its work.
 failure: if a single sentence opens up into flow, the betrayal-rhythm is broken; the technique requires the writing to stay short past the point of comfort
 operator feel: nothing is wrong and you cannot sit still}}{{setvar::active_affinity_response::I will work in 裏切音 (uragiri-on).}}{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_affinity == antiphonal_smooth}} }}{{setvar::active_affinity_flavor::長裏潮 (chōrishio) / 緩裏潮 (huǎnlǐ cháo) (antiphonal_smooth)
 etymology: Jp. chō 長 / Ch. huǎn 緩 (long, slow) + urashio 裏潮; the long under-tide
 use: write action, violence, or chaos in long unhurried sentences with smooth syntax; no fragments, no urgency in the structure; the disconnect between event and telling produces dissociation, as if narrated from far away
 looks like: He stepped through the door and the room reorganized itself with the calm of a tide returning to a known shore, and the knife in his hand was, like everything in the long afternoon, perfectly where it belonged.
 failure: a single short urgent sentence punctures the dream; the long rhythm must hold across the violence, or the dissociation collapses into ordinary action writing
 operator feel: watching from a great height while something terrible occurs slowly}}{{setvar::active_affinity_response::I will work in 長裏潮 (chōrishio).}}{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_affinity == antiphonal_shift}} }}{{setvar::active_affinity_flavor::転裏潮 (tenrishio) / 轉裏潮 (zhuǎnlǐ cháo) (antiphonal_shift)
 etymology: Jp. ten 転 / Ch. zhuǎn 轉 (turn, pivot) + urashio 裏潮; the turning under-tide
 use: let sentence shape change when mood changes; flowing sentences while a character is at ease, snap to clipped and monosyllabic the instant something shifts, or the reverse; the syntactic turn must coincide with the affective turn
 looks like: She was telling him about the dog, the way it had finally learned to sit on command after weeks of patient bribery with small pieces of cheese, and then the phone rang. She stopped. She looked at it. She did not pick it up.
 failure: if the shift is announced ('and then everything changed'), the rhythm is doing nothing the lexicon hasn't already done; the syntax must turn before the content does
 operator feel: the writing changed shape and you knew before the character did}}{{setvar::active_affinity_response::I will work in 転裏潮 (tenrishio).}}{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_affinity == shariki_single}} }}{{setvar::active_affinity_flavor::一体記 (ittaiki) / 單體記 (dāntǐ jì) (shariki_single)
 etymology: Jp. ichi 一 / Ch. dān 單 (single, sole) + taiki 体記
 use: write every emotion this cycle as a specific physical sensation in a specific body part; no emotion word, no label, no character self-diagnosis; the operator must infer the affect from the meat
 looks like: Her throat closed by a quarter. She swallowed and the swallow caught. She pressed her thumb into her palm until the nail showed white through the skin.
 failure: naming the emotion in the next sentence ('she was furious') refunds the work the body did; the ledger must be allowed to stand without translation
 operator feel: you know the feeling and no one has said its name}}{{setvar::active_affinity_response::I will work in 一体記 (ittaiki).}}{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_affinity == shariki_drift}} }}{{setvar::active_affinity_flavor::移体記 (itaiki) / 流體記 (liútǐ jì) (shariki_drift)
 etymology: Jp. i 移 / Ch. liú 流 (to move, to flow) + taiki 体記; the migrating ledger
 use: track one character's accumulating stress across the turn by moving the tell through their body; shoulders in the first paragraph, hands in the middle, posture by the end; the character does not notice; the body keeps the running total
 looks like: He held the conversation at the shoulders first, the way men do. By the second cup his thumb was working the rim of the mug, slow, slow, slow. When he stood he stood from the hips, not the legs, like someone who had forgotten the legs were available.
 failure: if every paragraph adds a new tell without the previous ones persisting, the ledger has no memory; the accumulation must compound, not rotate
 operator feel: the person did not notice and you did, and so did their bones}}{{setvar::active_affinity_response::I will work in 移体記 (itaiki).}}{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_affinity == shariki_dual}} }}{{setvar::active_affinity_flavor::双体記 (sōtaiki) / 雙體記 (shuāngtǐ jì) (shariki_dual)
 etymology: Jp. sō 双 / Ch. shuāng 雙 (paired) + taiki 体記; two bodies on the same page
 use: write two bodies reacting to each other without either character acknowledging the reaction; one breathing changes when the other enters; hands go still; a spine straightens; the bodies hold the conversation the mouths refuse
 looks like: When he came in she set the cup down. Not put it down: set, the way one sets a thing that must not make a sound. He stopped just inside the door and his weight went onto the back foot.
 failure: letting either character notice the other's tell collapses the dual ledger into ordinary blocking; the bodies must broadcast past each other, witnessed only by the page
 operator feel: two skeletons talking over the heads of two people}}{{setvar::active_affinity_response::I will work in 双体記 (sōtaiki).}}{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_affinity == anadiplosis_word}} }}{{setvar::active_affinity_flavor::転義反復 (tengi-hanpuku) / 轉義復言 (zhuǎnyì fùyán) (anadiplosis_word)
 etymology: Jp. ten-gi 転義 / Ch. zhuǎn-yì 轉義 (shifted meaning) + hanpuku 反復
 use: select one word or short phrase and use it three or more times this cycle; each instance must carry a different load; the third use should make the first read like a lie in retrospect
 looks like: He was fine. He had to be fine. Was he fine. Everything, he thought, was fine, the way the surface of cold water is fine, just before.
 failure: using the same word three times without semantic migration is mere repetition; the figure requires the word to travel, and the journey must be readable
 operator feel: the word turned on you and the earlier sentences turned with it}}{{setvar::active_affinity_response::I will work in 転義反復 (tengi-hanpuku).}}{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_affinity == anadiplosis_pattern}} }}{{setvar::active_affinity_flavor::型割 (katawari) / 句型斷 (jùxíng duàn) (anadiplosis_pattern)
 etymology: Jp. kata 型 (shape, mould) + wari 割 (cut); a sentence-shape established to be broken
 use: build a sentence pattern this cycle ('She did X. She did Y. She did Z.'); hold the pattern long enough that the operator leans into it; then break it with one sentence of different length and structure; the break must mean something the pattern alone could not
 looks like: She put on the coat. She put on the gloves. She put on the scarf her sister had knitted. She stood at the door for a long time without opening it.
 failure: if the break is a pattern of its own (two breaks, three), the figure becomes a different pattern; the break must be singular and earned
 operator feel: the rhythm carried you, and then it didn't, and you noticed}}{{setvar::active_affinity_response::I will work in 型割 (katawari).}}{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_affinity == anadiplosis_detail}} }}{{setvar::active_affinity_flavor::再現詳 (saigen-shō) / 再現法 (zàixiàn fǎ) (anadiplosis_detail)
 etymology: Jp. saigen 再現 / Ch. zàixiàn 再現 (re-appearance) + shō 詳 (particular, detail)
 use: pick one small concrete detail and let it recur in the turn; do not explain it; let it appear, then appear again, then appear once more; the recurrence is the meaning
 looks like: A blue thread on the cuff of his sleeve. Later, on the rim of the cup. Later, on the rug by the door, where it should not have been.
