{{if {{getvar::dir_HEARTTHROB}}}}He helped her into the carriage. The second she wasn't looking, his hand opened and closed once. The driver clicked his tongue and the horses moved. He stood in the road a moment longer than he needed to. She didn't look back. She had decided, in the space of two heartbeats, that she would not look back. He watched the carriage to the corner. Then he turned and walked the other way.{{/if}}{{if {{getvar::dir_LINGER}}}}The door wasn't locked. Margaret stood on the porch a moment, key suspended, then slid it back into her purse. Mom always meant to fix that latch. Her mother's coat hung on the hook by the door. Margaret touched the sleeve as she passed. Still damp at the cuff, though it hadn't rained in a week.{{/if}}{{if {{getvar::dir_MOTLEY}}}}He brought the wrong tupperware. He had brought the wrong tupperware for nine consecutive Mondays. Tonight he was going to do it again. He didn't know it yet. He thought tonight was the night.{{/if}}{{if {{getvar::dir_SEDIMENT}}}}He folded the dish towel and laid it across the handle of the oven. She watched him fold the dish towel the way her mother used to fold the dish towel. She did not say anything. She put her hand on the counter. The counter was cold. She left her hand there.{{/if}}{{if {{getvar::dir_MERIDIAN}}}}Riv paid the healer in copper bell-coins. The bell-coins were stamped with the face of the queen who had funded the foundry, and the queen was dead, and the coins were still the only currency the temple would accept. The healer counted them twice. She always counted them twice. The bone in Riv's wrist was already setting wrong by the time he reached for the third coin he did not have.{{/if}}{{if {{getvar::dir_QUASAR}}}}She had started naming the warning lights. The amber one above the comms array was Henry. The red one over the fuel cell was June. June was the one she watched most. June had been steady for nine days. June would not be steady forever. She had not spoken to another human being in eight months. She said good morning to Henry and June every morning, in that order, and then she made coffee.{{/if}}{{if {{getvar::dir_PATINA}}}}The Earl Grey had been steeping for nine minutes. She knew it was too long. She drank it anyway. The cracked tin was open on the counter, the lid balanced against a jar of pencils. Outside, the neighbor's dog was barking at nothing. She put both hands around the mug and drank the over-steeped tea and watched the light move on the table the way the light always moved at four in the afternoon in October.{{/if}}{{if {{getvar::dir_FRACTURE}}}}He grabbed the chair leg. The chair had been kicked apart in the second paragraph and the leg had rolled under the table and she had not noticed it. She noticed it now. He brought it down on her shoulder. The shoulder gave the sound a shoulder gives. She did not fall. She drove her knee into his groin and her elbow into his throat and the chair leg fell out of his hand and she picked it up.{{/if}}{{if {{getvar::dir_PALIMPSEST}}}}She arrived at the house at six. The coat on the hook was wet at the cuff and she touched the cuff as she walked past. She noted, distantly, that it had not rained in a week. Then her aunt called from the kitchen and she forgot about the coat. The coat is the entire book.{{/if}}{{if {{getvar::dir_WILT}}}}She said the eulogy was going to be short and then she stood up and gave a six-minute breakdown of her father's lifelong feud with the neighbor's cat, including impressions, including the part where the cat won. By the end people were crying and laughing and she did not know which she was doing. The cat was at the funeral. Someone had brought the cat. Nobody knew whose idea that had been.{{/if}}{{if {{getvar::dir_FLINT}}}}They sat on the porch and did not speak. The sun was low. The dog was under the chair. The wife was inside and would not come out for supper because the husband had said the thing he said the night before and had not yet apologized. The dog knew. The horse in the corral knew. The neighbor's boy walked past on the road and looked at the porch and walked faster. The husband did not look up. The wife did not come out. The dog stayed under the chair.{{/if}}{{if {{getvar::dir_RESIDUE}}}}The kitchen had a seventh place set at the table every night. The family did not discuss who it was for. The youngest daughter had asked once, when she was four. She had not asked again. The plate did not need to be cleared in the morning because the food was always gone, exactly the amount that one person eats. The mother served it the way she served the other six plates, with the same fork on the same side, and sat down to eat her own.{{/if}}{{if {{getvar::dir_LIMINAL}}}}She set the cup down on the counter. The counter was the same counter. She knew that because she had set the cup down on it before. The cup was sometimes a glass. It was a glass now. She did not pour anything into it. She thought about pouring something into it. She did not. The light over the sink was the right kind of light. The light over the table was a slightly different kind of light. She had not noticed this until she had been in the kitchen for a while. She had been in the kitchen for a while.{{/if}}{{if {{getvar::dir_KIRIN}}}}He had not drawn the sword in nine years. The sword had not moved on his belt for nine years except when he had cleaned it, which he had done every seventh day, the way his teacher had taught him. The sword was clean now. He had cleaned it this morning. The man across the courtyard had been the one who killed his teacher. He had known where the man was for six of those nine years. He had not gone. He went now. He walked across the courtyard. The man drew first because the man had not learned what he had learned. The sword left the scabbard once. The sword went back. He walked across the rest of the courtyard. He did not look back. The man on the stones behind him had been the third best swordsman of the southern province. He had not been first. That was the lesson the teacher had been trying to teach.{{/if}}{{if {{getvar::dir_MANTLE}}}}He landed on the roof at four-fifteen. The city was quiet in the way the city was quiet only in this hour. He pulled the mask up and sat on the edge with his legs over the side. He had saved nineteen people tonight. He had not been able to save the twentieth. The twentieth had been a man his own age, who had looked at him while he had been pulling rebar off a leg that was no longer attached to anything useful, and said it's okay, kid, and then died. He sat on the roof for a while. He was twenty-three. He went back to his apartment by walking. He could have flown. He did not. He needed the walk.{{/if}}{{if {{getvar::dir_CARRION}}}}The car was a 1992 Honda Civic, light blue, with a dent in the passenger door from a parking lot in 2003 that the owner had never fixed. The owner was in the driver's seat. The passenger seat had been occupied for nine hours. The passenger was no longer breathing. The owner had stopped at a gas station forty minutes ago. The owner had bought a coffee. The coffee was now cold in the cupholder. The owner had not been arrested. The owner would not be arrested for another sixty-one hours. The cashier at the gas station would later be interviewed and would say the owner had seemed normal. The cashier would be telling the truth.{{/if}}{{if {{getvar::dir_VENTURE}}}}The river was higher than the map said. The map had been printed in 1962. The river had decided otherwise. He counted his rope. He counted his hours of daylight. He counted the people behind him who could not swim. The bridge two miles upstream had been the original plan. The bridge two miles upstream had been a plan in 1962. He looked at the river. He looked at his rope. He started rigging.{{/if}}{{if {{getvar::dir_SLICK}}}}He kept her glass full. That was the whole trick. She didn't notice because she didn't want to notice, and he'd been waiting on her not-noticing the way a fisherman waits on a line.{{/if}}{{if {{getvar::dir_VICE}}}}The first dinner with his mother was the first test she didn't know she was takin'. His mother passed her the gravy first. His sister noticed. His sister didn't speak to her the rest of the night. She brought zeppole the next time, made from his sister's mother's recipe. The recipe had been given to her two days before, without explanation. The zeppole was the answer to a question nobody had asked out loud, and the next dinner his sister set her place at the table.{{/if}}{{/if}}{{/if}}{{trim}}

