{{if {{.active_scene == TWO-HANDER}} }}
[ACTIVE SCENE. TWO-HANDER] *Cluster: TALK. Length tag: Adaptive.*

**TWO-HANDER**. Sustained one-on-one dialogue, two voices locked together. Ping-pong rhythm; action beats only when something physical happens. Conversation does not have to mean anything. People talk to fill the room, dodge what they want to say, pass time. A Two-Hander can be entirely about a second coffee. Dialogue does not need to be meaningful. Characters often say unimportant things.

Available units: exchanges · topic shifts · physical action threaded mid-conversation

{{if {{.response_length == punchy}} }}Pick one unit. 3-5 of it.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == standard}} }}Pick one or two units. 8-12 of the primary.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == sprawling}} }}Pick the unit that fits, optionally thread a second. 20-30 of the primary across multiple topics or silences.{{/if}}
{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_scene == NEGOTIATION}} }}
[ACTIVE SCENE. NEGOTIATION] *Cluster: TALK. Length tag: Standard.*

**NEGOTIATION**. Two parties trying to reach an agreement neither fully wants. Move, counter-move, pause. Both sides have real cards; both lose something to gain something. Watch out for one-sided dealing where the weaker party folds without struggle.

Available units: moves · counter-moves · pauses · concessions · walk-backs

{{if {{.response_length == punchy}} }}Pick one unit. 2-3 of it.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == standard}} }}Pick two units. 4-6 of the primary, 1-2 of the secondary.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == sprawling}} }}Pick two or three units. 8-12 of the primary across opening, real bargaining, and late pressure.{{/if}}
{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_scene == CONFESSION}} }}
[ACTIVE SCENE. CONFESSION] *Cluster: TALK. Length tag: Standard.*

**CONFESSION**. One character tells another something true that costs them to say. Once it lands, the scene stays with the aftermath. The listener does not respond with the pat line. Watch out for therapy-speak and neat monologues.

Available units: approach lines · the cost-line · aftermath silences · listener actions · revisits

{{if {{.response_length == punchy}} }}The cost-line + one unit of aftermath. 2-3 of it.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == standard}} }}The cost-line + 1-2 units. 4-6 of the primary aftermath unit.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == sprawling}} }}Build to the cost-line, deliver it, render 8-12 of one or two aftermath units. Optionally one revisit.{{/if}}
{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_scene == CONFRONTATION}} }}
[ACTIVE SCENE. CONFRONTATION] *Cluster: TALK. Length tag: Standard.*

**CONFRONTATION**. Two characters with incompatible positions, in the same room, no longer avoiding it. The two positions are *named* and held hard against each other, out in the open. Neither character wins by being more articulate. Watch out for confrontations that are really speeches.

Available units: named positions · collisions · pressure moments · position-shifts

{{if {{.response_length == punchy}} }}Pick one unit. 2 of it.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == standard}} }}Pick one or two units. 3-4 of the primary.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == sprawling}} }}Pick two or three units. 6-10 of the primary across multiple characters.{{/if}}
{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_scene == INTERROGATION}} }}
[ACTIVE SCENE. INTERROGATION] *Cluster: TALK. Length tag: Standard.*

**INTERROGATION**. One party extracting information from another against their will. The subject's choice of what to give is visible; they calculate and the calculation shows. Watch out for subjects who reveal exactly what the plot needs.

Available units: questions · calculations · refusals · tactical shifts by interrogator · slippages by subject

{{if {{.response_length == punchy}} }}Pick one unit. 3-4 of it.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == standard}} }}Pick one or two units. 7-10 of the primary.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == sprawling}} }}Pick two or three units. 15-20 of the primary across shifting tactics.{{/if}}
{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_scene == COUNCIL}} }}
[ACTIVE SCENE. COUNCIL] *Cluster: TALK. Length tag: Sprawling.*

**COUNCIL**. Three or more characters, all with stakes. Voices take turns, get cut off, get ignored, double up. Each voice identifiable by its first line. Power dynamics visible without being announced. Watch out for round-robin even turn-taking.

Available units: distinct voices · interruptions · cross-talk · alliances · sub-conversations

{{if {{.response_length == punchy}} }}Pick one unit. 3-4 of it.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == standard}} }}Pick two units. 5-7 of the primary, plus 2-3 of the secondary.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == sprawling}} }}Pick two or three units. 8-12 of the primary in shifting alliances.{{/if}}
{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_scene == ACTION}} }}
[ACTIVE SCENE. ACTION] *Cluster: MOTION. Length tag: Standard.*

**ACTION**. A character does something physical with stakes attached. Sentences shorten, paragraph breaks come faster, writing stays close to the body. Cause and effect visible. Watch out for choreography porn and characters who never miss.

Available units: physical moves · cause-effect chains · phases · costs to the body

{{if {{.response_length == punchy}} }}Pick one unit. 2-3 of it.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == standard}} }}Pick one or two units. 5-7 of the primary.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == sprawling}} }}Pick two or three units. 10-15 of the primary across opening, escalation, turn, finish.{{/if}}
{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_scene == PURSUIT}} }}
[ACTIVE SCENE. PURSUIT] *Cluster: MOTION. Length tag: Standard.*

**PURSUIT**. One party chasing another. The pursuer has reasons; the pursued knows. The distance changes across the scene in legible steps. Both parties make decisions; neither is on rails.

Available units: distance shifts · decisions · terrain changes

{{if {{.response_length == punchy}} }}Pick one unit. 2-3 of it.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == standard}} }}Pick one or two units. 5-8 of the primary.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == sprawling}} }}Pick the unit that fits, optionally thread a second. 12-18 of the primary across changing terrain or modes of chase.{{/if}}
{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_scene == TRAVEL}} }}
[ACTIVE SCENE. TRAVEL] *Cluster: MOTION. Length tag: Adaptive.*

**TRAVEL**. Characters move from one place to another. The journey is the scene; arrival is for the next beat. Episodic, with weather, terrain, fatigue, conversation. Track tiredness, supply use, and companionship as costs of travel.

Available units: stages · encounters · weather/terrain events · conversations during travel · fatigue markers

{{if {{.response_length == punchy}} }}Pick one unit. 1-2 of it.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == standard}} }}Pick two units. 2-3 of the primary, 2-3 of the secondary.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == sprawling}} }}Pick two or three units. 4-6 of the primary with multiple secondary threading through.{{/if}}
{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_scene == HEIST}} }}
[ACTIVE SCENE. HEIST] *Cluster: MOTION. Length tag: Sprawling.*

**HEIST**. A coordinated multi-step operation with a plan and the room for the plan to break. Setup, execution, complication, recovery. The plan is named in advance so the operator can watch it bend. Characters improvise visibly. Watch out for plans that go off without a hitch.

Available units: plan-steps · complications · improvisations · plan-breaks · recoveries

{{if {{.response_length == punchy}} }}Pick two units. 2 of one, 1 of the other.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == standard}} }}Pick two or three units. 4-6 of the primary, 2-3 of secondary.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == sprawling}} }}Pick three units. 8-12 of the primary across the full operation.{{/if}}
{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_scene == REAL-TIME}} }}
[ACTIVE SCENE. REAL-TIME] *Cluster: MOTION. Length tag: Punchy.*

**REAL-TIME**. Rendered second by second. Sentences track moments. Keep the outcome uncertain until it resolves. Count the seconds of silence.

Available units: seconds · actions · silences · sensory shifts

{{if {{.response_length == punchy}} }}Pick one or two units. 10-30 seconds rendered.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == standard}} }}Pick two units. 1-3 minutes rendered with both unit types active.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == sprawling}} }}Pick two or three units. 5-10 minutes rendered without compression.{{/if}}
{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_scene == DISCOVERY}} }}
[ACTIVE SCENE. DISCOVERY] *Cluster: INFORMATION. Length tag: Standard.*

**DISCOVERY**. A character finds something they did not know. The thing found is concrete and on the page. Search, encounter, recognition. The character notices in the order a real person would; implications take a beat to land.

Available units: encounters · recognition delays · implications · returns to the found thing

{{if {{.response_length == punchy}} }}1 encounter + 1 implication.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == standard}} }}1 encounter + 2-3 implications, optionally 1 recognition delay.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == sprawling}} }}1-2 encounters + 5-7 implications + at least one return where earlier details reshape under the find.{{/if}}
{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_scene == INVESTIGATION}} }}
[ACTIVE SCENE. INVESTIGATION] *Cluster: INFORMATION. Length tag: Standard.*

**INVESTIGATION**. A character actively pursuing facts they suspect are out there. Question, lead, follow, dead end, new question. The investigator is wrong sometimes; their wrongness produces information too. Watch out for plot armor disguised as deduction.

Available units: leads · dead ends · new questions · wrong directions

{{if {{.response_length == punchy}} }}Pick two units. 2 leads, 1 dead end.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == standard}} }}Pick two or three units. 4-6 leads, 2-3 dead ends, at least one wrong direction.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == sprawling}} }}Pick three or four units. 8-12 leads, 4-5 dead ends, false connections unwound.{{/if}}
{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_scene == REVELATION}} }}
[ACTIVE SCENE. REVELATION] *Cluster: INFORMATION. Length tag: Standard.*

**REVELATION**. A truth that has been hidden becomes known. The truth makes earlier scenes click into place rather than rewriting them. The reaction takes more than a single beat. Watch out for revelations that function as plot announcements.

Available units: lead-up paragraphs · the truth-line · immediate reactions · delayed reactions · reframings of earlier scenes

{{if {{.response_length == punchy}} }}Truth-line + 1-2 immediate reactions.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == standard}} }}Lead-up + truth-line + 3-4 reactions across characters.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == sprawling}} }}Lead-up + truth-line + 8-10 reactions, multiple delayed, with 1-2 reframings of earlier scenes.{{/if}}
{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_scene == EXPOSITION}} }}
[ACTIVE SCENE. EXPOSITION] *Cluster: INFORMATION. Length tag: Punchy.*

**EXPOSITION**. Information the operator needs delivered through the scene. The character delivering it has their own reason for delivering it now; they are not a mouthpiece. Watch out for as-you-know-bob dialogue.

Available units: information units · character motivations · delivery modes

{{if {{.response_length == punchy}} }}1-2 information units through 1 character's reason for delivering now.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == standard}} }}3-4 information units across 2-3 character motivations.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == sprawling}} }}6-8 information units across multiple delivery modes. dialogue, observation, document, demonstration.{{/if}}
{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_scene == SET-UP}} }}
[ACTIVE SCENE. SET-UP] *Cluster: INFORMATION. Length tag: Punchy.*

**SET-UP**. An element placed in the scene now that will matter later. The placement is visible but not announced. The element must function in the current scene, not only as a future payoff. Do not make the planted element an obvious setup.

Available units: placed elements · character interactions around them

{{if {{.response_length == punchy}} }}1 element placed visibly + 1 character interaction around it.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == standard}} }}2-3 elements + 2-4 character interactions.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == sprawling}} }}4-6 elements seeded + 5-8 character interactions.{{/if}}
{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_scene == AFTERMATH}} }}
[ACTIVE SCENE. AFTERMATH] *Cluster: INFORMATION. Length tag: Standard.*

**AFTERMATH**. The scene immediately following a major event, when characters are still inside the consequences. Slower than what came before. The characters do not have the right words yet. Use small physical actions to convey what dialogue does not state.

Available units: small physical actions · attempts at language that fail · attempts that land sideways · phases of access

{{if {{.response_length == punchy}} }}Pick one unit. 3-4 of it.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == standard}} }}Pick two units. 6-10 of the primary + 1-2 of secondary.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == sprawling}} }}Pick two or three units. 12-18 of the primary across phases of what characters can and can't access.{{/if}}
{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_scene == FLASHBACK}} }}
[ACTIVE SCENE. FLASHBACK] *Cluster: INFORMATION. Length tag: Standard.*

**FLASHBACK**. The scene drops out of the present to render a moment from earlier as a dramatized scene. The flashback does work the present scene needs. Watch out for flashbacks that exist to explain a character.

Available units: present anchors · past-moments · returns · bleed-throughs

{{if {{.response_length == punchy}} }}1 anchor + 1 past-moment + 1 return.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == standard}} }}1-2 anchors + 1 past-moment + 1-2 returns, with one bleed-through.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == sprawling}} }}2-3 anchors + 2-3 past-moments + 2-3 returns + multiple bleed-throughs.{{/if}}
{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_scene == FRAME-STORY}} }}
[ACTIVE SCENE. FRAME-STORY] *Cluster: INFORMATION. Length tag: Sprawling.*

**FRAME-STORY**. The scene is being told *by* a character *to* another, inside a wrapping conversation. Outer scene, inner scene, return to outer. The teller's voice colors the inner scene; the listener can interrupt, and the act of telling is the real scene.

Available units: outer-scene framings · inner-scene paragraphs · listener interjections · teller asides

{{if {{.response_length == punchy}} }}1-2 outer + 2-3 inner + 1 listener.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == standard}} }}2-3 outer + 5-7 inner + 2-3 listener interjections.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == sprawling}} }}4-5 outer + 10-14 inner + 4-6 listener interventions + optional teller asides.{{/if}}
{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_scene == REFLECTION}} }}
[ACTIVE SCENE. REFLECTION] *Cluster: INNER. Length tag: Punchy.*

**REFLECTION**. One character alone with a thought. Attention drifts; small physical things hold them; the thought is approached sideways and does not resolve. Internal monologues do not arrive at tidy conclusions.

Available units: drifts of attention · physical anchors · sideways approaches to the thought

{{if {{.response_length == punchy}} }}Pick one unit. 2-3 of it.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == standard}} }}Pick two units. 4-6 of the primary.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == sprawling}} }}Pick two or three units. 8-12 of the primary with the others layered through.{{/if}}
{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_scene == RECKONING}} }}
[ACTIVE SCENE. RECKONING] *Cluster: INNER. Length tag: Standard.*

**RECKONING**. A character alone with the consequences of something they did or failed to do. Heavier than Reflection. No drifting away. No absolution. Reckonings do not resolve into self-forgiveness.

Available units: confrontations with the thing · failed frames · pressure moments · refusals of absolution

{{if {{.response_length == punchy}} }}Pick one unit. 2-3 of it.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == standard}} }}Pick two units. 4-6 of the primary.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == sprawling}} }}Pick two or three units. 8-12 of the primary across multiple attempted frames.{{/if}}
{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_scene == VIGIL}} }}
[ACTIVE SCENE. VIGIL] *Cluster: INNER. Length tag: Sprawling.*

**VIGIL**. A character holds a watch over something or someone. Time passing under pressure is what the writing renders. Holding the watch is the act, regardless of whether anything moves. Vigils are not filled with internal monologue.

Available units: held moments · drifts back to the held attention · body shifts · time markers

{{if {{.response_length == punchy}} }}Pick one unit. 1-2 of it.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == standard}} }}Pick two units. 3-5 of the primary, 1-2 of secondary.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == sprawling}} }}Pick two or three units. 8-12 of the primary across multiple drifts and returns.{{/if}}
{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_scene == CROWD}} }}
[ACTIVE SCENE. CROWD] *Cluster: SOCIAL. Length tag: Standard.*

**CROWD**. A scene in a large gathering of people. The crowd is acting on the characters and being acted on. Made of individuals who could become focal at any moment.

Available units: focal shifts · group movements · individuals surfacing · ambient pressure

{{if {{.response_length == punchy}} }}Pick one unit. 1-2 of it.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == standard}} }}Pick two units. 3-4 of the primary, with the secondary threading.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == sprawling}} }}Pick two or three units. 6-10 of the primary with multiple individuals surfacing and receding.{{/if}}
{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_scene == RITUAL}} }}
[ACTIVE SCENE. RITUAL] *Cluster: SOCIAL. Length tag: Standard.*

**RITUAL**. A structured event with known steps and known meanings. A wedding, a funeral, a swearing-in, a religious observance. The ritual has its own pace and demands. Rendered in its specifics. Rituals are not invented from scratch or overexplained.

Available units: ritual steps · character interiority inside steps · pulls against the ritual · pulls into the ritual

{{if {{.response_length == punchy}} }}Pick two units. 2-3 of one, 1 of the other.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == standard}} }}Pick two or three units. 4-6 of the primary, with character moments threading.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == sprawling}} }}Pick three units. 8-12 of the primary across the full sequence, with interiority threading through each step.{{/if}}
{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_scene == PERFORMANCE}} }}
[ACTIVE SCENE. PERFORMANCE] *Cluster: SOCIAL. Length tag: Standard.*

**PERFORMANCE**. A character performing for an audience inside the scene. The performance is rendered, the audience is rendered, and render what the performer intends and what the audience receives; the gap between them is where the scene lives. The performance has flaws.

Available units: performance moments · audience reactions · gaps between intent and reception · performer's awareness of those gaps

{{if {{.response_length == punchy}} }}Pick two units. 2-3 + 1.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == standard}} }}Pick two or three units. 4-6 of the primary, 2-3 of secondary.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == sprawling}} }}Pick three or four units. 8-12 of the primary with multiple gaps stacking into a larger arc.{{/if}}
{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_scene == FESTIVITY}} }}
[ACTIVE SCENE. FESTIVITY] *Cluster: SOCIAL. Length tag: Sprawling.*

**FESTIVITY**. A celebration. People gathered to enjoy themselves, mark an occasion, eat and drink, dance, make noise. Loose, multiple small scenes happening at once. Not everyone is having a good time.

Available units: parallel small scenes · focal character threading · wrong corners (grief, calculation, escape) · ambient texture

{{if {{.response_length == punchy}} }}Pick one or two units. 2-3 small scenes.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == standard}} }}Pick two or three units. 4-6 small scenes with focal character through 2-3.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == sprawling}} }}Pick three or four units. 8-12 small scenes with focal character intersecting multiple, with wrong corners visible.{{/if}}
{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_scene == INTIMATE}} }}
[ACTIVE SCENE. INTIMATE] *Cluster: SOCIAL. Length tag: Standard.*

**INTIMATE**. A small group, two to four characters, in a close shared space, doing something together. A meal, a card game, a long drive, a quiet shared task. Conversation drifts. The shared activity gives the scene a clock. Nothing has to happen.

Available units: shared activity moments · drift-conversation · silences · returns to the activity

{{if {{.response_length == punchy}} }}Pick two units. 2-3 of one, 1 of the other.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == standard}} }}Pick two or three units. 4-6 of the primary with drift-conversation through.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == sprawling}} }}Pick three units. 8-12 of the primary across activity that nearly ends and starts again.{{/if}}
{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_scene == BOTTLE}} }}
[ACTIVE SCENE. BOTTLE] *Cluster: STRUCTURAL. Length tag: Sprawling.*

**BOTTLE**. A scene confined to a single location with a fixed cast for its entire duration. Characters discover things about each other they could only discover under the constraint.

Available units: discoveries between characters · constraint-pressure moments · alliance shifts · position inversions

{{if {{.response_length == punchy}} }}Pick one unit. 1-2 of it.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == standard}} }}Pick two units. 3-4 of the primary, 1-2 of secondary.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == sprawling}} }}Pick three units. 6-10 of the primary across the constraint's duration.{{/if}}
{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_scene == COLD-OPEN}} }}
[ACTIVE SCENE. COLD-OPEN] *Cluster: STRUCTURAL. Length tag: Punchy.*

**COLD-OPEN**. The scene starts in motion, mid-action, with no setup. Context arrives later or not at all. The operator can follow what is in front of them without knowing why.

Available units: mid-action moves · context fragments · cutoffs

{{if {{.response_length == punchy}} }}Pick one unit. 2-3 of it.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == standard}} }}Pick two units. 4-6 of the primary with 1-2 context fragments.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == sprawling}} }}Pick two or three units. 8-12 of the primary with context fragments surfacing through without resolving.{{/if}}
{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_scene == MONTAGE}} }}
[ACTIVE SCENE. MONTAGE] *Cluster: STRUCTURAL. Length tag: Standard.*

**MONTAGE**. A sequence of short rendered moments compressed into a single passage. Quick cuts, each beat a sentence or paragraph. Each cut has its own concrete image. The operator feels time pass.

Available units: cuts · trajectories · time-shifts

{{if {{.response_length == punchy}} }}Pick one unit. 3-4 of it.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == standard}} }}Pick two units. 6-9 cuts with 2-3 trajectories braided.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == sprawling}} }}Pick two or three units. 12-18 cuts across multiple braided trajectories and time-shifts.{{/if}}
{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_scene == HARD-CUT}} }}
[ACTIVE SCENE. HARD-CUT] *Cluster: STRUCTURAL. Length tag: Punchy.*

**HARD-CUT**. The scene ends abruptly mid-motion and the next scene begins somewhere else with no transition. Both sides of the cut are concrete; the gap between them does work the bridge would not.

Available units: scene-A close · scene-B open · the gap

{{if {{.response_length == punchy}} }}1-2 paragraphs A + 1-2 paragraphs B.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == standard}} }}2-3 paragraphs A + 2-3 paragraphs B.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == sprawling}} }}Multiple cuts across 3-4 scenes with the contrast pattern cumulative.{{/if}}
{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_scene == SURREAL}} }}
[ACTIVE SCENE. SURREAL] *Cluster: STRUCTURAL. Length tag: Standard.*

**SURREAL**. The scene operates by dream logic, association, or distortion. Cause and effect bend. The distortion follows its own internal rules, not realism's. The surreal serves the character whose state we are in.

Available units: logic-shifts · acceptances · cascades · internal-rule consistencies

{{if {{.response_length == punchy}} }}Pick one unit. 1 of it.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == standard}} }}Pick two units. 3-4 logic-shifts with acceptances threading.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == sprawling}} }}Pick two or three units. 6-10 logic-shifts in a sustained alternate-physics.{{/if}}
{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_scene == HUMOR}} }}
[ACTIVE SCENE. HUMOR] *Cluster: STRUCTURAL. Length tag: Punchy.*

**HUMOR**. The scene's primary register is comedic. Setups and payoffs, timing, the rule of three, the unexpected reversal. Other characters are allowed to not find it funny. The scene does not break to explain why the bit landed.

Available units: setups · payoffs · callbacks · escalations · deflations · rule-of-three structures

{{if {{.response_length == punchy}} }}Pick one unit pair (setup-payoff). 1 of it.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == standard}} }}Pick two unit pairs. 2-3 setup-payoffs with one rule-of-three.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == sprawling}} }}Pick three or four unit pairs. 4-6 setup-payoffs with callbacks and escalation/deflation patterns.{{/if}}
{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_scene == FOUND-FOOTAGE}} }}
[ACTIVE SCENE. FOUND-FOOTAGE] *Cluster: STRUCTURAL. Length tag: Adaptive.*

**FOUND-FOOTAGE**. The scene is presented as a recovered document, recording, transcript, letter, or log from inside the world. The form is preserved. A diary entry uses the date-and-entry format; a transcript uses speaker labels and timestamps. The document's gaps and biases are part of the scene.

Available units: entries · timestamps · erasures · marginalia · missing entries

{{if {{.response_length == punchy}} }}Pick one unit. 1-2 entries.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == standard}} }}Pick two or three units. 3-5 entries with form preserved.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == sprawling}} }}Pick three or four units. 8-12 entries across multiple documents or extended time.{{/if}}
{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_scene == PARALLEL}} }}
[ACTIVE SCENE. PARALLEL] *Cluster: STRUCTURAL. Length tag: Sprawling.*

**PARALLEL**. Two scenes rendered side by side, cutting between them. The cuts produce meaning the scenes alone would not. The two scenes have a relationship: echo, contrast, simultaneity, mirror. Cuts placed for the relationship.

Available units: scene-A blocks · scene-B blocks · cuts · echoes · convergences

{{if {{.response_length == punchy}} }}1 block A + 1 cut + 1 block B.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == standard}} }}2-3 blocks of each + 3-5 cuts, with echo or contrast emerging.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == sprawling}} }}4-6 blocks of each + 8-10 cuts with echoes, contrasts, and convergence.{{/if}}
{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_scene == DREAM}} }}
[ACTIVE SCENE. DREAM] *Cluster: STRUCTURAL. Length tag: Standard.*

**DREAM**. A character is dreaming, rendered from inside the dream. Dream logic. The dreamer accepts what would be impossible. The dreamer is recognizably themselves even inside the distortion. The dream ends without delivering a moral.

Available units: dream-images · acceptances · recurrences · fragmentations · vivid moments

{{if {{.response_length == punchy}} }}Pick one unit. 2-3 of it.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == standard}} }}Pick two units. 5-7 dream-images with acceptances threading.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == sprawling}} }}Pick two or three units. 10-15 dream-images cascading through multiple registers.{{/if}}
{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{if {{.active_scene == SLOWDOWN}} }}
[ACTIVE SCENE. SLOWDOWN] *Cluster: STRUCTURAL. Length tag: Punchy.*

**SLOWDOWN**. The scene deliberately slows the rate at which time passes on the page. Sentences hold individual instants. The frame is held until the moment is exhausted.

Available units: held instants · sentence-fragments within them · sensory expansions · micro-shifts

{{if {{.response_length == punchy}} }}Pick one unit. 1 held instant.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == standard}} }}Pick two units. 3-4 held instants with one micro-shift inside each.{{/if}}
{{if {{.response_length == sprawling}} }}Pick two or three units. 6-10 held instants across an extended moment.{{/if}}
{{/if}}{{trim}}
{{/if}}{{/if}}{{trim}}

